2026-11-11
- dtmail, dtmailpr: remove the message "The dtmail program is not running as
  group \"mail\"" and the texts around it, which nothing has shown since the
  mailer stopped needing the mail group. `DTME_BadRunGroup` is gone from
  `DTMailError_t`, from the fall-back texts of `libDtMail` and from set 1 of
  `libDtMail.msg`, and message 4 ("Mailer has not been properly installed,
  and cannot run because the execution group is incorrectly set") is gone from
  set 2 of `DtMail.msg`; the two help sections of the Mailer volume that
  explained them, *Mailer has not been properly installed* and *Exit Mail?*,
  are gone from the volume of every locale. The catalogs are indexed by number
  (`libDtMail` looks an error up as message "enumerator + 1", `dtmail` names
  messages of `DtMail.msg` in its `CATGETS` calls), so every message after a
  removed one has a new number: the English sources, the sources of the
  locales and the 31 calls that named a message of set 2 were renumbered
  together, with no entry left in the place of the old one. A catalog that
  was installed before this change shows the wrong text under many errors
  and mailer messages until it is installed again. A new check,
  `libDtMail/tests/check-message-catalogs.py` (run by `make check`), reads
  every catalog source against the enumeration, the fall-back texts and the
  calls (same numbers, same error named above each message, same conversions,
  the installed locales with exactly the English set of messages), runs
  gencat on every source and reads each compiled catalog back, and looks every
  error up through the production `DtMailEnv` under the sanitizers with the
  English catalog, with each installed locale and without a catalog; it fails
  on the tree before this change and on eighteen deliberately broken copies
  of the catalogs, the code and the help volumes.
- Documentation: every manual page, guide, example, demo and message catalog
  shows the calls of the Desktop interface the way `include/Tt/tttk.h` declares
  them. The Japanese guides (`ttGuide`, the programmer's overview and the guide to
  `dtksh`) still showed `ttdt_session_join()` with five arguments and
  `ttdt_sender_imprint_on()` with nine; they now show the `requests` and
  `imprints` arguments with their explanation and the checks on the values, as
  the English ones do. The English synopses in `Tttttk.sgm`, the example of the
  media message set and the pages of the requests (`Get_Environment`,
  `Set_Locale` and the others) were brought up to date as well, the programmer's
  overview no longer asks every application to handle the requests that act on
  the whole process, and the guide shows `tttk_block_while()` with its
  `XtAppContext`. The usage of the `dtksh` command `ttdt_session_join` in the
  message catalogs of all nine locales names the optional requests (the French
  catalog had translated the name of the command). `make -C lib/tt/lib check`
  runs `check-documented-calls.py`, which compares the arguments of every call of
  a function of `tttk.h` in `doc/`, `examples/` and the demos with its
  declaration, and the usage of the `dtksh` command with `requests`; it fails 22
  of the places in the documents from before.
- libtt: `ttdt_sender_imprint_on()` changes the calling process only in the ways
  the caller names. The function put the locale, the working directory and (when
  the caller passed no place to return the display in) `$DISPLAY` of whoever
  answered into its own process, sent `Get_Geometry`, `Get_XInfo` and `Get_Locale`
  to no one in particular whenever it was not given a handler (the documented
  address to the sender of the contract was never made, so any program of the
  session could answer), and used what came back without looking at it. It has a new argument after the contract, a `Ttdt_sender_imprints`
  that is zero or the bitwise OR of `TTDT_IMPRINT_LOCALE`, `_SITUATION` and
  `_DISPLAY` (no value stands for all of them; `TT_ERR_NUM` for any other).
  Only the requests that are needed are sent, always to one handler or to the
  sender of the contract (`TT_ERR_POINTER` without either), and the display and
  geometry that the caller asks for are returned whether or not a change is
  named. The replies are checked (locales that cannot name a path, absolute
  directories, displays that are a host or a socket and a number, window sizes
  of 16 bits, no null byte inside a string, bounded lengths); a reply that fails
  makes the call change and return nothing and answer `TT_DESKTOP_EPROTO` (or
  `_EINVAL`), a request that the handler fails is reported with its status and
  the other replies are used. The replies are put into the process after the
  wait, and a request that was not answered by then loses its callback: a reply
  that came late used to be written into the stack of the call that had
  returned. The `CoEd` demo names all three imprints. Covered by the new sender
  imprint test of `make -C lib/tt/bin/ttsession check` (all 27 checks fail
  against the previous library); described in `docs/improvements/tooltalk.md`.
- libtt: the Desktop requests that a program has asked to answer check what
  they carry. `Set_Environment` no longer sets any variable a message sends:
  `PATH`, `HOME`, `LD_*`, `DYLD_*`, `TT_*` and the other names that
  `ttsession` does not pass on to the programs it starts (now one table,
  `util/tt_env_names.C`) fail with `TT_DESKTOP_EACCES`, names that are not shell
  names and values over 32768 bytes with `TT_DESKTOP_EINVAL`, and a request is
  checked as a whole before the first variable is set; a variable without a value
  is removed instead of set to the empty string. `Set_Locale` takes only the
  names of locales that cannot name a path, `Set_Situation` only absolute
  directories without control characters, `Signal` only numbers of signals,
  `Get_Environment` and `Get_Locale` only well formed names, and a string with a
  null byte inside or beyond its bound fails the request
  (`TT_DESKTOP_EPROTO`, `_EINVAL`); a locale the system does not have is
  reported as `TT_DESKTOP_EINVAL`. Covered by the session requests test of
  `make -C lib/tt/bin/ttsession check`, which sends 29 such requests to a
  process that asked for everything (the previous library failed 26 of the 29
  cases: it set `LD_PRELOAD`, accepted locales that name a path, and so on).
- libtt: the client decodes what it receives into its own classes. The library
  decoded the messages `ttsession` delivered, the messages dequeued from the
  database of a file and every file it looked up into the classes it shares with
  the server, and then called the operations of the client classes on them
  (`_Tt_c_message`, `_Tt_c_file`), which only worked because the client classes
  hold no data of their own. UBSan reported this for every message a client
  received (`next_message`, `lookup_mhandle` and `update_message`) and for every
  file-scoped operation. A message list is now decoded into a
  `_Tt_c_message_list` (`tt_c_xdr_next_message_args`, the client's counterpart of
  the server's xdr functions), the messages dequeued from a file likewise, and
  `find_file` makes `_Tt_c_file` objects; the casts are gone. The session
  requests test of `make -C lib/tt/bin/ttsession check` now fails on a
  sanitizer report from either of its processes, the library or `ttsession`,
  wherever it comes from (against the previous library every check that
  receives a message fails). File-scoped delivery, live and queued, was also run by hand against the
  sanitizer build of `rpc.ttdbserverd`: the previous library reports the
  confusion, this one reports nothing.
- libtt: `_Tt_string::cat()` no longer passes a null pointer to `memcpy()` for
  an empty string. `ttsession` reported it when a session ended, and it made the
  `table-directory` case of `make -C lib/tt/bin/ttdbserverd check` fail in a
  sanitizer build.
- libtt: the environment check of `make -C lib/tt/lib check` runs in a sanitizer
  build. It links the production sources in front of `libtt`, which holds the
  same globals; AddressSanitizer's duplicate-global check ended the test program
  before it listed its cases.
2026-11-10
- dtfile: build the directory control-packet test and the file conflict
  dialog placement code cleanly with GCC 14. The test program ended an `if`
  and a `return` on one line, which GCC's `-Werror=misleading-indentation`
  rejects, so `make check` stopped in `programs/dtfile` under GCC and skipped
  the checks after it; the conflict dialog's position clamping now has one
  statement per line. The full dtfile `check-local` passes under GCC 14.
- Build and CI: adopt CK Core Motif 44020a781 as the toolkit revision of this
  batch (`.ckgit/motif-revision` and the rpi4 sister pin). It releases the
  color server's property buffer once; the earlier revision aborted `dtwm` and
  every other client of a desktop session with a double free when it read that
  property. The revision also carries the toolkit's Editres, X reply, Xft
  colour cache, D-Bus and UID file hardening. The desktop smoke test and the
  matched native stack were validated against it, see
  `validation/2026-11-10-batch5-hardening.json`.
- libtt: `ttdt_session_join()` no longer registers, for every program that joins
  a session, a handler for the Desktop requests that act on the whole process.
  Any program of the session could set the environment of the joined program
  (`Set_Environment`: `HOME`, `PATH`, `TMPDIR`, `LD_*` reached its child
  processes and every `getenv()`), change its working directory
  (`Set_Situation`), send it a signal (`Signal`) or change its locale, read its
  variables, directory, locale and host (`Get_*`), and every program received
  `Do_Command` in its callback. The function has a new last argument, a
  `Ttdt_session_requests` that is zero or the bitwise OR of
  `TTDT_SESSION_GET_LOCALE`, `_SET_LOCALE`, `_GET_ENVIRONMENT`,
  `_SET_ENVIRONMENT`, `_GET_SITUATION`, `_SET_SITUATION`, `_SIGNAL`,
  `_GET_SYSINFO` and `_DO_COMMAND`; a request that is not asked for is not
  registered for and fails (`TT_ERR_NO_MATCH`, or `TT_DESKTOP_ENOTSUP` when it
  is addressed to the program). There is no old path. The call returns
  `TT_ERR_NUM` for an unknown value and `TT_ERR_POINTER` for `Do_Command` without
  a callback, destroys its patterns when the join fails, and returns the error of
  a pattern that could not be made instead of freeing it. `dtpad` in server mode
  asks for `Get_Sysinfo` (a second server asks the first for its host name),
  `ttsnoop` and the code generated by Application Builder for a `Do_Command`
  connection ask for `Do_Command`, `dtksh`'s `ttdt_session_join` takes the
  requests as further arguments, and every other program asks for none. Covered
  by the session requests test of `make -C lib/tt/bin/ttsession check`
  (it fails against the previous library); described in
  `docs/improvements/tooltalk.md`.
- libtt: the library's answer to a `Get_Locale` request now includes the locale
  of the category `LC_CTYPE`. The handler only answered categories with a value
  above zero, and `LC_CTYPE` is 0 on glibc, so that category was left out of
  every reply. Covered by the session requests test of
  `make -C lib/tt/bin/ttsession check`.
- dtmail, dtmailpr: run without the mail group. `dtmail` is no longer installed
  set-group-id; a new program, `dtmailspool` (plain C, nothing but the C
  library, installed with the `mail` group and mode 2755 by `make install`),
  does what needs the group in the mail spool directory
  (`--with-mail-spool-dir`, default `/var/mail`) for the user who started it
  and for nobody else: it opens or creates the user's own mailbox and hands the
  descriptor to the mailer, makes the file that replaces the mailbox in a save
  and renames it over the mailbox, and makes, checks, releases and breaks the
  `.lock` and `.dtmail` lock files. A request names no path; every file is
  reached relative to the spool directory without following links and must be a
  regular file of the user; the group is held for one operation at a time and
  every change is verified; the helper reads no environment, closes inherited
  descriptors, is not dumpable, ignores the signals of a terminal and the size
  limits of the mailer, and ends on any frame that is cut, too long,
  wrong or carries a descriptor. `libDtMail` talks to it through `SpoolHelper`
  and `MailboxFiles`, which also handle every other mailbox with plain system
  calls (no second attempt with a group). The mailbox descriptor is now
  close-on-exec, a read-write open of a mailbox that cannot be written falls
  back to read-only without an `access()` call, and the link count of a mailbox
  opened read-write is taken from the open descriptor (it was read from an
  uninitialised buffer). A helper that is not installed or does not start makes
  the inbox fail to open with an access error; there is no second attempt
  without it. Removed: `include/DtMail/MailGroup.hh` and its in-process group
  toggling (the `PRIV_ENABLED` macros and the enable/disable callbacks of
  `Session`, `Application` and `RoamApp`), `isSetMailGidNeeded()`,
  `GetGroupName()`, `GetIdForGroupName()` and the start-up check that `dtmail`
  runs as group `mail`. Covered by
  `programs/dtmail/tests/check-spool-helper.py` (the helper under the
  sanitizers: every truncated or oversize frame, descriptors, links,
  directories, FIFOs and files of other users, locks, the save, a killed
  helper, a fake kernel for the group ids, source checks) and
  `libDtMail/tests/check-spool-client.py` (replies that are cut or wrong, both
  ways of handling files, recognising the user's mailbox), plus new spool cases
  of `check-mailbox-files.py`; described in `docs/improvements/mail.md`.
- dtfile: complete background file-operation protocol writes and fail safely
  when their control channel is lost. Conflict replies, modification notices,
  errors, completion records, rename results and create results now use the
  shared EINTR-, short-write- and SIGPIPE-safe writer. A failed pre-mutation
  notice stops the affected filesystem change, failed completion publication
  makes the worker fail, and unresolved source-directory metadata no longer
  reaches `stat` through a null path. Covered by focused extracted-production
  packet, closed-reader and unresolved-path cases.
- dtfile: make conflict cancellation and multi-row failure outcomes terminal
  and deterministic. Cancelling a conflict batch now completes the operation
  with a cancelled failure status instead of success, and the worker retains
  the first row, protocol, transfer or cleanup failure rather than replacing
  it with a later error while the remaining rows drain.
- dtfile: harden workspace icon refresh after file moves. Source and target
  paths now use checked joins instead of fixed-buffer formatting, parent tests
  no longer copy arbitrary paths or underflow on empty input, prefix rewrites
  check their allocation arithmetic, and oversized paths skip only the visual
  refresh. Refreshed desktop metadata now replaces its owned strings once,
  including when optional help or restriction values are absent.
- dtfile: make destination path splitting transactional and capacity-aware.
  File creation and copy/move/link dialogs now pass explicit output sizes,
  correctly recognize a root source directory without reading an uninitialized
  destination buffer, preserve `/` as the parent of absolute root entries, and
  reject oversized hosts, directories, leaves and malformed trailing slashes
  before publishing partial outputs or starting an operation.
- dtmail: harden the POP2, POP3, APOP and IMAP clients against what a server
  sends. A command is formatted with `vsnprintf()` and refused when it does not
  fit or carries a line break (a password or folder name could run a second
  command); a line from the server is trimmed without reading before its start;
  a message count outside 0..1,048,576, the numbers of a size or `UIDL` list and
  an identifier longer than its buffer no longer size arrays, index them or
  overflow; the IMAP tag wraps instead of overflowing; reading a message
  restores `SIGINT` on every exit; the APOP client finishes its MD5 digest; host
  names resolve on 64-bit systems (every name was taken for 255.255.255.255);
  socket error texts are bounded and the second, 32-bit-only variant of the
  socket functions is gone; `SafeCtime()` ends its text inside its buffer and
  `GetGroupName()` takes the size of the caller's buffer. Covered by cases that run the clients against a
  scripted server on the loopback address and by
  `programs/dtmail/tests/check-mail-servers.py`; described in
  `docs/improvements/mail.md`.
- dtmail: put `message/partial` messages together safely, convert enriched text
  and build MIME and address text without buffers sized by a guess. Opening a
  mailbox that held the parts of one partial message stopped the mailer (a null
  parent in the header reader); part numbers and totals are read as plain
  numbers and limited to 1,000 parts and 256 MiB, and the text of the message
  grows as needed instead of being sized from `Content-Length` and appended with
  `strcat()`. A long command in enriched text no longer overflows the stack; MIME
  types, character sets, the `From ` line and address lists are bounded; lock
  files get random temporary names, the `.dtmail` lock is read into a
  terminated buffer, and `free()` and `delete[]` match their allocations.
  Covered by real-mailbox cases in `libDtMail/tests/mailbox-files.C` and by
  `programs/dtmail/tests/check-mail-messages.py`; described in
  `docs/improvements/mail.md`.
- dtfile: make copy, move and link dialog labels and destination paths
  independent of fixed localized-text assumptions. Selected-object labels now
  compose Motif strings directly, while rename, preview and resolved target
  paths use checked joins and reject values that do not fit before starting a
  file operation. Covered by focused path-boundary and source-contract cases.
- dtfile: bound the remaining directory refresh and attribute-display paths.
  Refresh checks and cached modified-subdirectory checks now reject child paths
  that do not fit instead of concatenating into fixed buffers or indexing before
  an empty directory name. Attribute rows bound account-service user and group
  names, link targets and every formatted output, and use a stable placeholder
  when local time conversion fails. Covered by focused directory path, time and
  source-contract cases.
- dtfile: bound the directory worker's file, symbolic-link and metadata text
  paths before copying them into fixed records. Oversized names, relative-link
  expansion, data types and action labels now fail without publishing a partial
  record; a parentless relative link no longer dereferences before its buffer,
  null data-type results fail safely, and root-directory metadata avoids a null
  name comparison. Directory message tags now require a complete read, and
  position-file initialization falls back to the numeric user ID when account
  lookup is unavailable. Covered by focused path/control and packet sanitizer
  cases.
- rpc.cmsd: an update request that carries an attribute with no value and a name
  the calendar has not seen no longer crashes the daemon (the type of the missing
  value was read); the attribute is skipped. One more case in
  `programs/dtcm/server/tests` (34 in all).
- libDtSvc and dtspcd: accept only a whole termios description. The string that
  carries the settings of a terminal must name the protocol version, have all
  eight fields and name only flags, speeds and control characters that exist,
  with control character values that fit in a byte; the settings change only
  then and a refused string is an error of the request (`dtspcd` answers with a
  protocol error). The error texts of the SPC library are built within their
  buffer and are no longer used as a format, so a long or odd name sent by a
  peer cannot overrun the buffer or act as a format. Covered by
  `lib/DtSvc/tests/spc-termio-test.c`.
- libDtSvc: read data types and database files within the data they have. File
  contents are matched within what could be read (a failed or short read no
  longer leaves unwritten memory in the comparison; sizes of zero or less, and
  offsets beyond the end or counted from the end, are handled), symbolic links
  are followed to their end with a limit and without dropping the last character
  of the target, the file names that replace `%file%`, `%name%` and the other
  keywords are quoted in commands and never searched for commands themselves,
  `%suffix%` and `%base%` belong to the file name, the cache file name is built
  from the display name safely and a cache header is checked before the file is
  mapped. The database file reader builds its messages in buffers that fit the
  names, no longer cuts the last character of a line without a newline, keeps
  more than ten variables, ends the expansion of variables that refer to
  themselves and searches the rest of a line after a multibyte character. New
  documentation page `docs/improvements/data-types.md`; covered by
  `make -C lib/DtSvc check`.
- dtlogin: a string resource that did not change is left alone when the
  configuration is reread (its size from the resource database counted the NUL
  and never matched the string). Covered by
  `programs/dtlogin/tests/check-config.py`.
- dtlogin: opening the display, the pseudo reset of the server, the server ping
  and the locale scan no longer use SIGALRM and a jump out of the interrupted
  code. The first two run in a thread with a time limit, the ping looks at the
  connection without waiting, and the locale scan checks a deadline between
  directory entries; the sort array of the language list is bounded. Covered
  by `programs/dtlogin/tests/check-x-timeouts.py`.
- dtlogin: the startup and setup scripts of a display end with the display when
  it is asked to stop (SIGTERM) instead of being waited for to their end; the
  reset script still runs to completion. Covered by
  `programs/dtlogin/tests/check-session-signals.py`.
- dtlogin: an XDMCP `Request` can only name the address it comes from (from the
  host itself, any address of the host); other requests are declined, so a
  display cannot point the manager at a third host. The reverse lookup of a
  client's address runs in a child process with a two second limit and its
  result is cached for a minute, instead of blocking the manager's main loop.
  Covered by `programs/dtlogin/tests/check-xdmcp.py`.
- dtlogin: the greeter reads the manager's requests completely and checks them.
  The length in the header is bounded by the 512 byte buffer, all of the
  message is read however the pipe splits it, the last byte is a NUL, and the
  offsets of message and challenge strings must lie inside the message; other
  requests are ignored. Covered by `programs/dtlogin/tests/check-greeter-request.py`.
- ttsession: a session that is told to end with `SIGTERM` or `SIGINT`, or whose
  process-tree root process exits, is taken out of the portmapper. The signal
  handler used to call `exit()` and left the registration behind; it now notes the
  signal and the main loop deletes the session and exits. Covered by
  `make -C lib/tt/bin/ttsession check`.
- rpc.ttdbserverd: a property no longer takes more values, a file or object more
  properties, and a file more objects than a reply can list: the request that
  would be one too many is refused with `TT_DB_ERR_DISK_FULL` (`TT_ERR_DBFULL`).
  The limit of the objects of a file is 65536 instead of 1048576. Listing the
  properties of a file or object no longer takes time quadratic in their number.
  Covered by the `list-limits-values`, `list-limits-properties` and
  `list-limits-objects` cases of `make -C lib/tt/bin/ttdbserverd check`.
- DtSearch: the exact search refuses a record number in the `.d99` file that the
  database cannot have, also the few between the last record and the end of its
  bit vector, which indexed the weight vector outside its storage. The unbuilt
  `lib/DtSearch/mrclean.c`, the predecessor of `dtsrclean`, is removed. Covered
  by `make -C programs/dtsr check`.
- libtt: the lock file of the authority file is created with a create that
  fails when the name exists and does not follow a link, and the file the new
  entries are written to is made the same way, so a link planted in the
  directory of the authority file cannot send the truncation or the write to
  another file. Covered by `make -C lib/tt/lib check`.
- rpc.cmsd: look at the request path again. The size and the check of a calendar
  and the registration of a callback (versions 4 and 5) now need rights to the
  calendar, the debug-only procedure that discarded a calendar's appointments
  in memory is gone, the text of a long recurrence rule is built in a buffer that
  grows with it instead of overrunning a fixed one, a calendar that cannot be set
  up because memory ran out is no longer freed twice, and a failed dump of a
  calendar closes its temporary log. Four new cases in
  `programs/dtcm/server/tests` (33 in all) fail on the previous sources.
- rpc.cmsd: the signal handlers no longer do the work. `SIGALRM`, `SIGUSR1`,
  `SIGUSR2` and `SIGHUP` only set flags; the daemon keeps them blocked and
  runs garbage collection (which pings the registered clients and rewrites
  the logs), the debug switch and the exit between two requests in its own
  service loop, instead of inside a handler that could interrupt the
  allocator, a request or the RPC library. It ignores `SIGPIPE`. Covered by
  `programs/dtcm/server/tests/loop-test.c`.
- rpc.cmsd and libcsa: bound every string, array and list of the calendar
  protocols. The XDR routines of versions 2 to 5 and of the callback protocol
  now apply named limits (`lib/csa/cmlimits.h`) to requests and replies, check
  the values of enumerations and the types of attribute values, send a missing
  string as the empty string, and code linked lists in a loop with the same
  bytes on the wire, so that a request with a very long list can no longer
  crash the daemon or make it allocate without bound. The routines of versions
  2 to 4 are maintained in `rtable{2,3,4}_xdr.c` instead of being generated.
  Covered by `make -C lib/csa check` (17 cases under the sanitizers).
- dtlogin: the servers file is read safely: over-long lines are ignored whole,
  a line that starts with a NUL byte is skipped, and a `servers` resource that
  leads to no file is reported instead of crashing the manager. The resource
  database loaded for a display no longer leaks the previous one. Covered by
  `programs/dtlogin/tests/check-config.py`.
- dtlogin: the process that manages a display no longer does its work in signal
  handlers. The handlers for the manager's SIGTERM and SIGHUP, the alarm and the
  X server's SIGUSR1 only leave a note; the waits for the greeter, for its end,
  for the user's session, for a process group that is stopped and for the
  server look at the notes with the signals blocked and sleep with
  `pselect`/`sigsuspend`, so a request is neither lost nor run on top of
  whatever the process was doing. A wait for a child that does not exist ends
  instead of looping, and a pending request does not cut short the wait for a
  stopped process group. Covered by `programs/dtlogin/tests/check-session-signals.py`.
- dtlogin: an expired password is changed in the login screen. The greeter
  puts the questions of the PAM password section (current and new password,
  messages of the modules) to the user, `libDtSvc` runs `pam_chauthtok` with a
  conversation of `dtlogin` (`_DtSvcPamChangePassword`), and the user logs in
  with the new password. Before, OK in the expired-password message logged the
  user in without a new password (the greeter replaced its exit status when no
  session was chosen), and the terminal for `passwd` it was meant to start
  needed an `xterm` at a fixed path; both are gone. The shipped PAM service
  file has a `pam_unix` password section instead of `pam_deny`. Covered by
  `programs/dtlogin/tests/check-password.py` and `lib/DtSvc/tests/check-svc-pam.py`.
- dtlogin: harden the readers of the configuration files. The `Xaccess` reader
  no longer overruns its word buffer, keeps the entries after one whose display
  does not resolve, matches display patterns in time proportional to their
  length and without reading past a trailing backslash, and looks up the local
  host address once (a host name that does not resolve no longer crashes the
  manager). The `Xservers` reader handles `local_uid` without a user, a display
  listed again with a class and a very long host name. The search for
  configuration files no longer opens what it finds; the language expansion
  of resource file names stops at a trailing `%`; the alternate desktop loader
  checks its allocations, limits the number of desktops and no longer edits the
  resource database it reads. Covered by `programs/dtlogin/tests/check-config.py`.
- dtlogin: an XDMCP `Manage` request no longer ends the session of a display
  that the configuration file lists or another host manages, merely by naming
  it; only the same host replaces its own session. A request that runs out of
  memory removes its half-built display from the list instead of freeing a
  record the list still pointed at. A display that cannot be created gives back
  its shared page, and removing a display releases its authorization names,
  name lengths and icon search paths. Covered by
  `programs/dtlogin/tests/check-displays.py`.
- dtlogin: the chooser reports its choice to the login manager through a Unix
  socket in the authorization directory (mode 0600, removed at shutdown,
  not inherited by sessions) instead of a TCP port open on every interface. A
  report is accepted for a client whose display runs a chooser, and the chosen
  host is used for the next query (before, every report was dropped). The list
  of indirect-query clients is bounded and expires, and the debug log of a
  report with a long client address no longer overflows its buffer. Covered by
  `programs/dtlogin/tests/check-chooser.py`.
- rpc.ttdbserverd: the properties that `TT_SET_OBJ_PROPS`, `TT_SET_OBJ_PROP`,
  `TT_ADD_OBJ_PROP` and `TT_DELETE_OBJ_PROP` answer with are freed after the
  reply is sent; the daemon kept them, and the delete answers with them on every
  call. The request tests of the daemon now run with the leak detector on.
- ttsession: the observers of a notice sent with the disposition `TT_QUEUE` or
  `TT_START` no longer get it a second time as a failed message. A notice has no
  handler to queue the message for or to start, and `ttsession` failed it for
  that; it now leaves the notice to its observers. Covered by
  `make -C lib/tt/bin/ttsession check`, which runs two clients against a session
  of its own.
- DtSearch: a statistical query ends with an error ("The index of the database
  is damaged") when an address list in the `.d99` file is longer than the
  database has objects, is cut off, or names an object that does not exist,
  instead of reading and writing outside the arrays of the query. `dtsrdelete`
  fills the positions it frees at the end of a list with ones. `dtsrhan` and
  `dtsrdbrec` no longer define a global (`shutdown_now`, `print_dbrec`) that the
  library defines too. Covered by `make -C programs/dtsr check`.
- rpc.ttdbserverd: changes of the properties of an object that was changed since
  the caller read them are refused. `TT_SET_OBJ_PROPS`, `TT_SET_OBJ_PROP` and
  `TT_ADD_OBJ_PROP` made the change although the cache level said it was out of
  date, and `TT_DELETE_OBJ_PROP` answered success without deleting; they now
  answer `TT_DB_ERR_UPDATE_CONFLICT` with the current properties, which
  `tt_spec_write` reports as `TT_ERR_DBUPDATE`. The groups an AUTH_UNIX
  credential lists besides the one the caller acts in now count for the access of
  files and objects, as they do for a file in a Unix file system. Covered by the
  `update-conflicts` and `supplementary-groups` cases of
  `make -C lib/tt/bin/ttdbserverd check`.
- libtt: a process that runs with other privileges than the user who started
  it (set-user-ID or set-group-ID programs) no longer reads `TTSESSION_CMD`,
  `TT_TRACE_SCRIPT`, `LD_LIBRARY_PATH`, `TTAUTHORITY`, `TTPATH`,
  `OPENWINHOME`, `DTMOUNTPOINT` and `DTAUTOMOUNTPOINT` from its environment, and
  takes the home directory from the password database. The `ttsession` that
  such a client starts runs with the real user and group ids, given up for good.
  `TTSESSION_CMD` is now a program with its arguments, not a line for a shell,
  and the display name reaches `ttsession` as one argument. The tools `ttcp`,
  `ttmv`, `ttrm`, `tttar` and `tt_type_comp` run `cp`, `mv`, `rm`, `tar` and the
  preprocessor directly, so file names with blanks and shell characters work and
  `tt_type_comp` no longer has a fixed limit on the length of its preprocessor
  options. The `ttrcp` source, which no build ever compiled and which built
  `rsh` command lines from file names, is removed. Covered by
  `make -C lib/tt/lib check` (sanitizer cases with a
  simulated and, started as the superuser, a real privileged process) and
  `make -C lib/tt/bin check`.
- dtmail: format the text of mail server errors and the lock dialog with
  `snprintf()`, and quote the sound file named by `realsound` in the mailrc
  as one argument for the shell. The answer of a POP3 or IMAP server no longer
  overflows the 2048-byte dialog buffer, and the command that plays a sound is
  no longer built in a fixed buffer from an unquoted name. Covered by
  `programs/dtmail/tests/check-message-text.py`; described in
  `docs/improvements/mail.md`.
- dtmail: handle the data of ToolTalk Mail, Display and Print requests safely.
  A Mail request without data no longer sends a message buffer that was never
  set; the file it names must be a regular file of at most 64 MiB, a FIFO is
  not waited for, and the file is closed and its buffer released correctly. A
  Display request without data is refused instead of passing a null mailbox
  name on, a Print request with a buffer uses the file made from it, and that
  file is created with `mkstemp()` and mode 0600 in `~/.dt/tmp` instead of by
  `tempnam()` (which fell back to `/tmp` without `$HOME`). Covered by
  `programs/dtmail/tests/check-tooltalk-handlers.py`; described in
  `docs/improvements/mail.md`.
- dtmail: bound what the user who starts the mailer controls. The font list
  built from `-xrm` resources, the exported `DISPLAY`, the words of a session
  file, the messages about an attachment file given with `-c` or a ToolTalk
  message and the widget names of the header lines of the compose window no
  longer overflow buffers of a fixed size (they matter where dtmail is
  installed set-group-id). The session file is accessible to its owner only.
  Covered by `programs/dtmail/tests/check-local-input.py`; described in
  `docs/improvements/mail.md`.
- dtmail, dtmailpr: build the text made from a received message without
  fixed buffers. The address list of a reply, the subject of a reply to a
  message without one, the headers written by "Save As Text", the header line
  of dtmailpr, the character set name of a message and the name of a print file
  made from the subject no longer overflow buffers sized by a guess; they use
  the new `include/DtMail/TextBuffer.hh` or bounded copies. `phrase()` allocates
  the terminator it writes and frees its buffer; printing or saving more
  messages than the array holds uses the first ones instead of writing past it.
  A date in the subject of a reply stops at the first line break. Covered by
  `programs/dtmail/tests/check-message-text.py` (the production functions under
  the sanitizers and source guards); described in `docs/improvements/mail.md`.
- libDtMail: create the temporary file of a mailbox save exclusively, resolve
  links safely and stop starting a shell to expand paths. The file next to
  the mailbox is now made with `mkstemp()` (it was a predictable name opened
  with `O_CREAT|O_TRUNC` while the mail group was held); a mailbox that is a
  link is resolved with `realpath()` (the old loop read link targets into a
  pointer-sized buffer and copied them into fixed ones); `expandPath()` and
  `getRelativePath()` expand `$NAME` and `${NAME}` themselves instead of running
  `echo` through `popen()`, and refuse a path of 2048 bytes or more instead of
  writing past their buffers. The `Session` callbacks for the mail group, the
  busy state and the interaction time start as null. Shell syntax other than
  variables is no longer expanded in mailbox and folder names. Covered by
  `programs/dtmail/libDtMail/tests/check-mailbox-files.py` (real mailboxes
  opened and saved under the sanitizers); described in
  `docs/improvements/mail.md`.
- dtmail, dtmailpr: give up the mail group of a set-group-id installation
  before the environment is read, and verify every change of the effective
  group. The group is dropped first in `RoamApp::initialize()` (it was dropped
  after the locale and the CDE environment were set up) and the drop and the
  switch back for spool operations are done in one place,
  `include/DtMail/MailGroup.hh`, which checks `getegid()` after `setegid()` and
  stops the program when the group cannot be given up (the results of
  `setgid()` were ignored). Covered by
  `programs/dtmail/tests/check-group-privileges.py` (fake-kernel scenarios under
  AddressSanitizer and a source guard); described in
  `docs/improvements/mail.md`.
- dtfile: remove the last raw, pointer-bearing `FileData` directory-worker
  packet. Removed-entry tombstones are now packed into bounded `FileData2`
  records and sent with complete writes; oversized or incomplete records fail
  before publication, and the obsolete short-read decoder is gone. Covered by
  focused production-helper sanitizer cases.
- dtfile: replace the desktop-refresh worker's raw `FileData` pointer-bearing
  records with validated `FileData2` packets. Desktop refresh now rejects
  truncated strings, records and tags transactionally, checks ToolTalk path
  resolution before filesystem access, uses complete writes for every packet,
  retires malformed workers, and resets the activity originally scheduled
  rather than mutable directory state. Covered by focused production-helper
  sanitizer cases.
- dtfile: harden the remaining directory status channels. Position-save and
  periodic timer results now use complete writes and exact, transactional
  reads; incomplete or invalid timer replies retire desynchronized sticky
  workers instead of returning them to the reuse pool. Link-check path joins
  are bounded, and failed position-file path resolution no longer reaches
  file I/O through a null pointer. Missing position files are treated as an
  already-complete removal, and access failures never report stale zero
  `errno`. Covered by the directory control-packet production helper cases.
- dtfile: complete the directory worker control-channel validation. Path-type
  and position-info arrays are now decoded into bounded temporary storage and
  replace cached state only after every count, string, coordinate, and control
  field is complete. Negative or excessive counts, missing mandatory strings,
  truncated records, unknown tags, and short DONE/ERROR payloads terminate the
  worker safely while preserving the prior directory state. Producers now use
  exact writes for path, position, DONE, and ERROR packets and stop on failure.
  Position files no longer allocate directly from an unchecked declared count,
  strip the last byte from an unterminated name, narrow out-of-range X11
  coordinates, or turn malformed records into an empty update that discards
  cached positions. Their path construction is now bounded as well. A failed
  ToolTalk path or path-component resolution is rejected and released before
  dereference, and a non-searchable update directory reports `EACCES` instead
  of stale `errno` state.
  Covered by the production helper cases in
  `programs/dtfile/tests/check-directory-control-packets.py`.
- dtfile: validate batched directory-entry packets before they reach cached
  directory state. The worker now reserves its packet header, aligns record
  storage, bounds aggregate text before copying, encodes update flags without
  signed-short overflow, initializes transmitted records, avoids inspecting
  failed `lstat` output, and completes packet and single-record writes or
  stops. The reader requires complete headers and payloads, bounds record
  counts and packet sizes, rejects negative, truncated, trailing, or
  inconsistent field lengths, and decodes a whole batch transactionally.
  Malformed responses discard pending replacement data and preserve the
  previously displayed directory instead of overflowing stack buffers,
  walking beyond a packet, or installing a partial result. Non-pipe callers
  retain valid oversized metadata through the unpacked reader. Covered by the
  production pack/decode cases and producer/consumer contracts in
  `programs/dtfile/tests/check-directory-packets.py`.
- dtfile: harden the remaining Trash size, move, empty, and attribute-change
  worker channels. Their producers now complete fixed fields or stop, and
  their callbacks require complete tags, scalars, result arrays, and mandatory
  paths before changing Trash, cache, or dialog state. Invalid request counts
  are rejected before callback-array allocation. Oversized result counts,
  truncated arrays, extra move results, and malformed responses fail the
  pending items, release the Xt input, and show protocol feedback instead of
  indexing past an operation, treating zero-filled results as successful
  deletion, or leaving an operation hung. A status-returning string decoder
  now distinguishes a valid empty optional field from a truncated field. The
  recursive-size child now uses `_exit`, and the shared exact-read and
  protocol-error helpers are available to every dtfile pipe consumer. Covered
  by the string-field and Trash index/result cases and
  callback/producer contracts in
  `programs/dtfile/tests/check-transfer-completion.py`.
- dtfile: reject malformed legacy worker responses before they reach file,
  directory-cache, or widget state. Exact reads now reject negative sizes and
  truncated fields; string writes reject lengths that cannot fit the protocol
  and complete under a thread-local SIGPIPE scope. Move/copy update indexes are
  checked against the operation before indexing, and move/copy, rename, and
  create callbacks treat an incomplete tag, scalar, string, or unknown message
  as a failed response with user feedback and normal ownership cleanup. Create
  completion also frees destination strings when no view callback receives
  them. Covered by the pipe and process cases in
  `programs/dtfile/tests/check-transfer-completion.py` and
  `check-process-lifecycle.py`.
- dtfile: make file-operation startup transactional. All worker channels are
  checked, close-on-exec, and cleared before close; interrupted setup syscalls
  retry safely, a second-channel or fork failure closes every acquired
  descriptor, reverses directory-cache reservations, releases callback state,
  and shows the user why the operation did not start. Forked workers use
  `_exit` and a synchronous `select` failure hands the live operation to the Xt
  event loop instead of abandoning it. Move/copy status is now per operation,
  so concurrent callbacks cannot inherit another operation's error state.
  Invalid or overflowing request arrays are rejected before cache or process
  state changes. Covered by `programs/dtfile/tests/check-process-lifecycle.py`.
- dtfile: replace the single/global overwrite response with a bounded per-item
  conflict review. Each exact native source and destination remains separate
  from its escaped display text; malformed, oversized, truncated, duplicated,
  or stale requests fail without publishing a partial choice. Copy, move, link,
  and dropped-buffer conflicts can independently replace, keep both, rename the
  destination, rename the incoming object, merge compatible folders, or skip.
  Literal rename components reject empty names, slashes, `.` and `..`; applying
  an action changes only compatible rows. The event loop reads bounded request
  increments, close/cancel owns and closes its reply endpoint, and dialog and
  help lifetimes remain valid until the worker completes.
- dtfile: keep conflict filesystem work in the background worker. The UI only
  returns typed choices and cached open-view constraints; it no longer deletes
  or renames destinations. Before an existing object is changed, the worker
  revalidates the exact source and destination identities captured at discovery.
  Replace retains a temporary backup until the incoming operation succeeds,
  attempts restoration on failure, and reports any backup that must be kept for
  recovery. This is dtfile's compatibility path, not an fs-lib execution
  receipt; the unqualified fs-lib Apply path remains disabled. Covered by
  `programs/dtfile/tests/check-collision-workflow.py`.

2026-11-04
- libDtSvc, dtlogin: check the account and keep attempts apart in the PAM
  helper that dtlogin authenticates with. `_DtSvcPamAuthenticate` now calls
  `pam_acct_mgmt` after `pam_authenticate` (an expired or locked account or a
  `pam_nologin` refusal stopped nobody; a password that must be changed is
  reported as `PAM_NEW_AUTHTOK_REQD`, as dtlogin already expected), starts every
  authentication from a new handle (the user of an earlier failed attempt stayed
  in the handle and was the one whose password the next attempt checked), ends
  the handle of a refused login, no longer reads an uninitialized status or
  sets `PAM_USER` only when something failed, and keeps the password pointer
  only for the call. The conversation function reads the messages as the array
  of pointers PAM passes instead of an array of structs, fails a visible
  question or a missing password instead of answering with nothing, and frees
  what it allocated when it fails. Covered by `lib/DtSvc/tests/check-svc-pam.py`.
- dtsession: install without setuid, and keep a startup guard for the installed
  identity. `dtsession` no longer needs root (it was installed setuid root where
  there is no PAM, to read the password database): the `install-exec-hook` of its
  Makefile is removed, and so are `SmPrivRaise`, `SmPrivLower`, the saved
  set-user-ID that kept the way back to root, the group switch for console
  management and the drop that the started clients made before their exec.
  `SmPrivDropInstalledIdentity()` remains as the first statement of `main()`,
  before the locale or the environment is touched: if the effective user or
  group id differs from the real one (an installation made setuid or setgid by
  hand) it gives the installed identity up for good, tries to take it back to
  prove that it cannot, and exits with a message when a step is refused, has no
  effect or leaves a way back (an installation that is not setuid or setgid, and
  a `dtsession` started by root, make no identity call). Removed with them:
  `GetMemoryUtilization()` (it read `/dev/kmem` through an HP-UX kernel symbol
  table), the `system` value of `contManagement`, the unused `memThreshold`
  resource, the AIX high-function-terminal ring code that was never compiled and
  the Tru64 `SIA` code. Covered by `programs/dtsession/tests/check-privileges.py`
  (fake kernel).
- dtsession: unlock the screen only with the password of the user who started
  the session, checked through PAM. The root password and the passwords of the
  users named in the `keys` resource no longer unlock the display, and the
  resource is removed. The account is the one of the real user ID, never typed
  into the dialog or taken from the environment; the lock dialog names that
  same account instead of `LOGNAME` (an unset `LOGNAME` ended `dtsession` with
  a null pointer). The PAM service is always `dtsession` and no longer follows
  `argv[0]` (a `dtsession` started through the search path failed every
  unlock); an expired, locked or refused account and an empty password are
  refused, and a password that expired while the user was logged in is not (the
  change belongs to the next login); a PAM module that asks for anything but
  the password fails the conversation; the typed password is wiped from memory
  after the check. The code that read the password database and called
  `crypt()` is removed, and with it the setuid installation of `dtsession` on
  systems without PAM: a `dtsession` built without PAM cannot lock the display
  (it says so in the error log and carries on). The `account` entry of the
  `dtsession` PAM service uses `pam_unix` (it was `pam_deny`, which the check
  of the account would have run into). Covered by
  `programs/dtsession/tests/check-pam-lock.py` and
  `check-lock-authentication.py`.
- dtsession: lock the display only when it can be unlocked, and say why when it
  cannot. At startup and before every lock request `dtsession` checks that it
  was built with PAM, that the file of the PAM service (`/etc/pam.d/dtsession`,
  `/usr/local/etc/pam.d/dtsession` on FreeBSD) exists and can be read, and that
  the user who started the session has an account. If not, the error log names
  the reason (the message for the service file names the file), the lock
  request is refused with a bell, and the session goes on. Before, a missing
  service file was found out when somebody tried to unlock, and a build without
  PAM reused the generic "Unable to lock display due to security restrictions"
  message, which is replaced by three specific ones. Covered by
  `programs/dtsession/tests/check-pam-lock.py` and
  `check-lock-authentication.py`.
- dtsession: carry out a `SIGTERM` shutdown from the event loop. The handler
  ran the complete shutdown (ToolTalk notices, saving the session, an X event
  loop, the `sessionexit` script) inside the signal handler, which deadlocks or
  corrupts memory when the signal interrupts `malloc` or Xlib. It now writes a
  byte to a pipe that the event loop reads; repeated signals cause one shutdown.
  The shutdown holds the signal while it runs and gives the signal mask back
  when a client cancels it (it stayed blocked, so the next logout signal was
  never delivered); the shutdown waits for the clients with `XtAppProcessEvent`
  (with `XtAppNextEvent` a cancel or the timeout was not noticed until an X event
  arrived); a request made during a shutdown is dropped; if the signal
  cannot be set up it is logged and `SIGTERM` keeps its default action. Covered
  by `programs/dtsession/tests/check-signals.py`.
- dtsession: handle the session directories without a shell. Removing and
  copying a session ran `rm -rf` and `cp -r` through `system()` with the home
  directory unquoted, and moving one started `mv` through the search path; they
  are done in process now (symbolic links are not followed, a move onto an
  existing directory fails instead of nesting). The error log is rotated again
  (the file name was built one character short, so `errorlog` never became
  `errorlog.old`), by renaming instead of link and unlink so that a file system
  without hard links does not lose it, and a long `HOME` no longer corrupts the
  heap. A home directory too long for the session paths, or one whose
  `.dt` directories cannot be created, no longer overflows the path buffers or
  dereferences a null path at startup; `dtsession` starts with the system
  defaults. A save archives and, when a write fails or the save is cancelled,
  restores the directory it actually wrote (a Home session that saves into
  `current` at logout used to be replaced by the archive, and the `sessionetc`
  script was taken for an archive after the first save); the temporary name for
  an older archive is made with `mkdtemp`; pruning only goes on once the session
  was moved aside; removing a directory carries on past an entry it cannot
  remove, and a failed session copy is removed. Covered by
  `programs/dtsession/tests/check-session-files.py` and `check-session-save.py`.
- dtsession: read saved sessions and palettes within the bounds of their
  buffers. The quoting parser of the CDE 1.0 client file overran its nesting
  table; the hint, command and remote command buffers overflowed on lines longer
  than 5,000 bytes (and by a byte before that); options without a value
  dereferenced a null pointer and the host of one line leaked into the next;
  the saved font path overran its array at 50 entries, the key mapping divided
  by zero and the modifier map was written without regard to its size.
  `Dtstyle.paletteDirectories` with more than one directory hung the color
  server, long entries overran the stack and a long directory name the heap, and
  palette files with more colors than the table, long lines or too few colors
  read and wrote outside their buffers. The unused `MigrateResources` converter
  is removed. Covered by `programs/dtsession/tests/check-session-parsers.py` and
  `check-palette-parser.py`.
- dtsession: fix crashes in the XSMP session management callbacks. Registering
  a client that restarts with the id it had before (the `previousId` of a
  restored session) compared it with the null id of the connection that was
  registering and ended `dtsession`; deleting the first property of a client
  used the freed list head; the list of clients from the window manager was
  walked as far as its count said, whatever it held. Covered by
  `programs/dtsession/tests/check-xsmp.py`.
- dtsession: start clients, the window manager and the resource loader with
  `fork()` instead of `vfork()`. The saved environment of a restored client was
  put into the environment of `dtsession` itself (the child shares its memory
  until the exec) and reached every client started after it; a failed exec ended
  in `SM_EXIT`, which returns while the session is being saved, so the child
  went on as a second `dtsession`. The child now ends with `_exit`. Covered by
  `programs/dtsession/tests/check-client-start.py`.
- dtsession: leave the lock files of the ICE authority file alone when the lock
  cannot be taken. The failed lock removed the lock files of the process that
  held it, then wrote the session's cookies to a second file in the system
  configuration directory (`/etc/dt.ICEauthority`, where no client looks) and
  leaked a buffer; only `~/.ICEauthority` is written now. A cookie that cannot be
  made no longer leaves uninitialized entries for the exit handler to free.
  Covered by `programs/dtsession/tests/check-ice-authority.py`.
- dtsession: fix the life of XSMP client records. A client that disconnected
  after asking to interact left its request behind, so the window manager's
  reply made `dtsession` tell a closed connection to interact (and, with
  restart hint "never", used the freed record); records of clients that asked
  not to be restarted were never removed because the match ran after their
  connection was set to null, and neither they nor the properties' names and
  types were freed; registration leaked the previous id, the generated id and
  the host name. Covered by `programs/dtsession/tests/check-xsmp.py`.
- dtsession: load the language and resolution independent resources without
  assuming they are there. An unset `LANG` or saved language reached `strcmp`
  as a null pointer, a long `LANG` overran the `strncat` chain that builds the
  system font file name, a session without directories was formatted into the
  name, a missing `*fontList` resource handed the toolkit an uninitialized
  pointer and a missing `RESOURCE_MANAGER` property a null database. Covered by
  `programs/dtsession/tests/check-startup-resources.py`.
- dtsession: remove two `DEBUG`-only code paths: the trace that appended the
  user's resource database to the fixed file `/tmp/dtsession.xrm` (no
  `O_NOFOLLOW`, a directory every user can write to) and the endless loop in
  `main()` for more than five arguments. `programs/dtsession/tests/check-debug-code.py`
  guards both.
- build: add the opt-in `--enable-hardening` and `--enable-sanitizers[=LIST]`
  configure switches. Hardening reports truncation, string-constant,
  ignored-result, null-dereference and format defects and adds stack protection
  and fortified libc calls; sanitizers build and link with
  `-fsanitize=address,undefined` by default. Ordinary builds are unchanged.
- docs: record the first hardening warning and cppcheck baseline and a
  per-component audit ledger, and add `scripts/warning-baseline.py` to compare
  a build's warning counts with that baseline.
- dtspcd, DtSvc: validate SPC requests before using them. The request buffer
  holds a full-size payload and its terminating NUL, and a payload above
  `SPC_BUFSIZ` ends the connection instead of overrunning the buffer. Headers
  must be fixed-width hexadecimal and name a defined request type; the
  dispatcher no longer indexes one entry past its table or calls a type with no
  handler; a request that needs a channel and names none is answered with an
  error. Counted-string and multi-packet readers work within the payload and
  reject malformed, short or oversized counts instead of reading stale buffer
  bytes or writing outside their vector. The termios, debug-file and
  application-spawn handlers check their fields and bound their buffers.
  Channel ids come from a counter, not the channel address.
- dtspcd: register a user fail-closed. The authentication file name has a random
  suffix, must be a regular file with a single link, and its owner must match
  the user named; the daemon verifies that it gave up root (groups, group,
  user, and that root cannot be regained) and exits if any step fails, and a
  home directory or shell that does not fit is refused. A failed `initgroups`
  is logged correctly instead of passing an integer to `%s`.
- dtspcd: add request-level regression tests (`make -C programs/dtspcd check`)
  that link the built `libDtSvc` and run under the sanitizers.
- DtSvc: fix a heap overflow in data-type lookup. The scratch buffer for
  database attribute text never grew past 100 bytes, so a name pattern, content
  test or type name of 100 bytes or more overflowed it, and attribute values of
  1024 bytes or more overflowed the buffer that expands them. Both buffers now
  grow to the value and report allocation failure. Content tests,
  `.DtDirDataType` parsing, `%dir%`/`%name%`/`%base%` expansion of names without
  a directory, backquote expansion (which now closes its pipe and no longer uses
  command text as a format), attribute and type-name lists (`DtDtsFindAttribute`
  overran its list when every type matched) and lookup state are bounded or
  checked as well. Sanitizer-built regression tests run under `make check` in
  `lib/DtSvc`.
- dtterm: when installed setuid root, give up root for good before starting the
  shell and stop if that does not take effect; a user at a process limit could
  previously get a shell with root privileges. Returning the window process to
  the user identity is checked the same way. A test with a fake kernel covers
  refused and ignored changes and a remaining way back.
- dtcm, libcsa: harden the calendar manager request path. Log records, entry
  tables and attribute lists are built in buffers that grow with the data,
  escaped, and read back by a lexer sized by the file; calendar names are
  copied without a fixed buffer, and an empty name or one containing '/' is
  refused; the operator array of lookup, archive and restore requests no longer
  overwrites the attribute count; attribute numbers sent by clients are
  resolved by name and bounded; use-after-free in calendar creation and
  old-format entry update, deletion of several appointments, re-creation of an
  existing calendar, and root privilege after a failed chmod or chown are
  fixed; the garbage-collection signals are held back while a request runs.
- dtcm: add request-level regression tests (`make -C programs/dtcm/server
  check`) that include the production log writer and request handlers and run
  under the sanitizers.
- dtlogin: harden XDMCP, authorization and signal handling. A Request offering
  only loopback or malformed addresses no longer indexes past its arrays,
  XDM-AUTHORIZATION-1 without the handshake is declined, and authorization
  cookies come from /dev/urandom instead of the clock. The Willing status, xrdb
  run, backup and server authority files no longer use temporary names,
  redirects or a shell. Signal handlers set flags and the main loop acts on
  them, failed privilege drops stop the server or session, and sanitizer tests
  run under `make check`.
- scripts/warning-baseline.py: compare a build with the assembled validation
  record as well as with a bare `record` output.
- dtterm: when installed setuid root, return to the user's identity as the first
  thing main() does. Until the first terminal widget existed, the toolkit read
  resource files and search paths named by the caller's environment, such as
  XENVIRONMENT, with the installed identity, so a user could have a file only
  root can read parsed as resources. A source guard checks the order.
- tt: harden ttsession's decoding of requests from clients that are not yet
  authenticated. Strings, byte strings and string lists with a negative or
  oversized length, session addresses with long host names, null objects and
  truncated bodies no longer crash the daemon or overrun buffers (one RPC call
  with a huge cookie length killed ttsession before). The session cookie comes
  from `/dev/urandom` only, and no session starts if it cannot be read.
- tt: add sanitizer tests for ttsession's decoding and request handling that
  run the production code (`make -C lib/tt/slib check`).
- tt: apply ttsession's authentication level to every request. At the unix and
  des levels a request with no credentials was served; it is now refused, as is
  a request with credentials too weak for the level. A failed secure-RPC
  credential lookup refuses the request, ttsession refuses `-a des` in a build
  without secure RPC, the cookie is compared without stopping at the first
  difference, and the unix and none levels log a warning when ttsession starts.
- tt: limit the environment variables a message can set for a started ptype. The
  variables ttsession sets itself win over those of a message, names that change
  which code a program runs, whom it believes it is or how it finds its session
  are not passed on, and `TOOLTALK_START_ENV` lets the owner of the session
  approve names or restrict the set to a list. A variable that cannot be set
  stops the start.
- tt: harden rpc.ttdbserverd against malformed requests. Descriptors outside
  the table of open files are refused, a NetISAM descriptor beyond the table
  is closed (an open that finds nothing to close fails instead of looping), and
  a failed `isopen` or `isbuild` no longer keeps a descriptor. A transaction
  with a stale descriptor no longer closes a server descriptor or removes an
  earlier log, and the log is opened without following links with its record
  lengths checked. Records from clients are copied into zero-padded buffers;
  missing key descriptors, short object keys, oversized session ids, empty or
  table-less paths, and message info or cache levels stored as properties are
  checked; `iscntl` replies from writable storage; `TT_GET_ALL_SESSIONS` no
  longer takes a NetISAM position from the wire; transaction record lists are
  decoded without recursion; requests that fail to decode are freed.
- tt: keep the record lengths that `_Tt_isam_file` and `_Tt_client_isam_file`
  read from the file (they were reset to zero afterwards, so every record
  buffer held one byte), and move overlapping b-tree entries in mini_isam with
  `memmove`.
- tt: add sanitizer tests that run the real dispatcher of rpc.ttdbserverd on
  made-up requests (`make -C lib/tt/bin/ttdbserverd check`).
- tt: make NetISAM and its users agree on the width of a long. On Linux the
  feature macros of the build hide `LONG_BIT` from glibc's `<limits.h>` in C
  code while the C++ compilers show it, and the headers fell back to 32, so on
  a 64-bit build `libisam` stored 4-byte longs where rpc.ttdbserverd and libtt
  sized keys and records for 8-byte ones: the daemon could not build the tables
  of a partition (it ended in the second index of the first table), and a
  stored -1 came back as 4294967295. `isam.h`, `tt_client_isam.h`,
  `tt_db_server_consts.h` and `tt_old_db_consts.h` now take the fallback from
  `ULONG_MAX`, so 32-bit builds are unchanged and 64-bit builds use 8-byte
  longs in C and in C++. `stfloat` stores a float at its own width, not at the
  width of a long. `make -C lib/tt/mini_isam check` runs the check as C and as
  C++ against `libisam`, and the partition cases of the rpc.ttdbserverd tests
  no longer skip.
- DtSearch: give a database one layout on every machine, in C and in C++.
  `LONG`, `ULONG`, `DB_ADDR` and `F_ADDR` are 8 bytes (with the 2-byte `INT`
  and the 4-byte fields of the records), defined with fixed-width integers and
  asserted in `Dt/vista.h` and `Dt/Search.h` instead of taken from `LONG_BIT`,
  which glibc hides from C code built with the feature macros of the build:
  the engine (built with `_XOPEN_SOURCE`), the rest of the library and the C++
  code of DtInfo disagreed on their size. Page and record headers, the set and
  member pointers, the key pages, the data dictionary (`tiny.c`) and the
  entries of the `.d99` file follow, and the dictionary version is `V4.dt`, so
  a database of the earlier layout is refused ("incompatible dictionary file")
  and has to be made again. The comparison of a key reads its slot by offsets
  instead of through a misaligned structure.
- DtSearch: `dtsrclean` and `dtsrdelete` no longer die of a signal when they
  print their first message (they wrote to a stream that only the engine's
  start-up sets), `dtsrdelete` keeps the entries of an address list as they
  are in the file (it wrote them back in host order, which left every list of
  a word it changed unreadable on a little-endian machine), and it prints the
  record count with the right format.
- DtSearch: add tests. `make -C lib/DtSearch check` builds the widths of the
  types and the layout constants as C and as C++; `make -C programs/dtsr check`
  makes a database of 420 documents with the tools, reads its `.d99` file for
  8-byte entries, queries it through the DtSrAPI (single and several words,
  and, or, not, exact, stemmed and statistical search, under the sanitizers
  when the compiler has them), and queries it again after `dtsrclean -v` and
  after `dtsrdelete`.
- tt: keep the cache levels of rpc.ttdbserverd from overflowing. The level of a
  file or object is the value of a property that clients can set to any `int`,
  and counting it up, or counting the level a client sent in a request for the
  objects of a file, overflowed a signed int. A level at the highest `int` is
  now refused as a malformed level, and a client that sends it is told that
  nothing is newer.
- tt: bound the size of every string, byte array and list of the rpc.ttdbserverd
  protocol. A request of 16 bytes could demand a string or list of up to 4 GiB;
  the XDR routines of the server and libtt now refuse lengths above named limits
  (`TT_DB_LIMIT_*` in `lib/db/db_server.h`: paths and file names by
  `MAXPATHLEN`, property names 64, property values 1 MiB, message bodies
  16 MiB, lists of 1024 to 1048576 entries), including the paths of the old
  `iserase`, `_TT_MFS` and `TT_GET_FILE_PARTITION` requests. A property name
  above 64 characters, which the server cut off without saying so, is refused.
  A string a reply has none of (the name of a missing property, the forward
  pointer of an object that has none) is sent as the empty string instead of
  failing to encode. The tests have the allocator refuse requests for more than
  64 MiB and decode every limit and the size above it.
- tt: make rpc.ttdbserverd check who reaches which database. The check that a
  user may read or write the properties of an object compared the name of the
  property table with the name the old servers gave it and never applied, so
  everybody had access to everything through the old interface; the protection
  of the file the object is in now decides, as rpc.ttdbserverd(1M) says (owner,
  group and other bits, both permissions for a request that reads and writes,
  every record of a transaction, `gettype` and the session procedures
  included). `isbuild`, `isopen` and `iserase` serve only a path
  `<partition>/TT_DB/<table>` in a partition of the host, never through a link
  and never a path that merely contains `TT_DB`. The partition in an object id
  has to be a mount point or a name of the partition map, so a client can no
  longer have a `TT_DB` directory made anywhere. `TT_IS_FILE_IN_DB` and
  `TT_IS_OBJ_IN_DB` run with the credentials of their own request and are
  refused without any, `getoidaccess` and `setoid*` refuse a descriptor another
  user opened, and `addsession`, `delsession` and `gettype` find the records
  they are about, `addsession` no longer stops at a session that differs from the
  new one and `delsession` no longer removes a session that differs from the
  named one.
- tt: do not dereference a missing mount point. `bestMatchToPath` of
  `_Tt_file_system` returned a null entry when no mount point leads to a path
  (an empty mount table, or a relative path after the working directory was
  removed), and its callers in libtt and rpc.ttdbserverd used it; they now treat
  the path as having no partition. `_Tt_dirname` and `_Tt_basename` no longer
  read before the start of a path that has no `/`. The unused `set_real_path`
  of rpc.ttdbserverd is removed.
- tt: the dispatcher of rpc.ttdbserverd converts the routine that decodes an
  argument once instead of three times, which takes the warning count of lib/tt
  back under its recorded baseline.
- tt: use the real length of a key in rpc.ttdbserverd. A key is the structure
  `_Tt_key_data`, 16 bytes on a 32-bit build and 32 bytes on a 64-bit one, but
  `OID_KEY_LENGTH` was 16, so on a 64-bit build the access check, the session
  procedures, `gettype`, the modification date, the message queue locks of the
  old interface and `TT_GET_ALL_SESSIONS` and `TT_DELETE_SESSION` of the new one
  looked for their records at the wrong offsets of the tables the server makes.
  `OID_KEY_LENGTH` is now the size of the structure, the access record has one
  layout (the 16 bit layout of the first servers is dropped), keys are zeroed
  before they are filled, and `ttdbck` follows. The tests make their databases
  through the requests of the new interface and read them through the old one.
- tt: fix the XDR routine for the access of a request (user, group and mode).
  It moved each field as a `long` through a pointer to a 4 byte field, so on a
  64-bit build it read and wrote 8 bytes of each and the last field ran 4 bytes
  past the end of the access, in libtt as well as in rpc.ttdbserverd. Each field
  now goes over the wire as a 32 bit word of its own, -1 as -1; the bytes on the
  wire are unchanged.
- tt: fix the access check of the requests of the new interface in
  rpc.ttdbserverd. A write was let through for a user whose group or whose
  others only had the read bit (the check fell through from the write bit to the
  read bit), and a member of the group of a file, refused by the group bits, got
  the bits of the others. The rule is now that of a file in a Unix file system
  and of the old interface: the owner and the superuser may do anything, anybody
  else is in the one class of the group or the others, a read needs the read bit
  of the class and a write the write bit. It is a method of `_Tt_db_access`
  (`permits`) that the server calls. Moving an object to another file needed
  only a read of the object and nothing of the file it moved to; it now needs a
  write of the object and of the file.
- tt: remove the NetISAM procedures of the old servers from rpc.ttdbserverd and
  libtt. The server answered the procedures 1 to 24 (`isopen`, `isbuild`,
  `isread`, `iswrite`, `isrewrec`, `isdelrec`, `isstart`, `iserase`, the oid
  access and session calls) on any table by its path, including the table that
  holds who may do what, and their access check followed the key a client sent
  and not the record it read or changed; no patch of that interface is complete,
  and nothing needs it. They are gone with their XDR routines, the cache of oid
  access information, the log of their transactions, the conversion of the
  tables of the old servers when a partition is opened, the `_Tt_old_db` and
  `_Tt_client_isam` classes of libtt and the fallback of `_Tt_db_client` to them,
  and a request for a procedure from 1 to 24 is answered as one for a procedure
  that does not exist. The table of the files the server has open moves to
  `tt_isam_cache.C`. The option `-n` now applies to the procedures that remain,
  which it did not before. The tests lose the cases of the removed procedures
  and gain one that the numbers 1 to 24 are refused and do nothing.
- tt: fix three crashes of rpc.ttdbserverd found by sending its procedures
  requests that were changed at random. After any request that returned a string
  or a list, `TT_GET_OBJ_TYPE` and `TT_GET_OBJ_FILE` for an object id of no
  partition answered with the string the dispatcher had freed, and freed it
  again; the dispatcher now clears every pointer it frees and the two procedures
  start with none. `_tt_base64_decode` read through a null pointer for an object
  id with no key in it and, for a character above 127, before its table. A file
  name without a `/` made `_tt_is_file_a_directory` compute a pointer from null.
  New tests send every procedure a successful request and then a failing one,
  object ids and file names that are not well formed, and (as before, for the
  procedures that remain) random changes of sound requests.
- tt: make rpc.ttdbserverd work only in a `TT_DB` directory that is its own. The
  daemon runs as root in partitions that users may write to and opened the
  tables of a partition by their paths after a `mkdir` that was not looked at,
  so a user could plant a `TT_DB` that was a link, or a directory of his own, or
  a table that was a link, and have the daemon make or read tables where he
  chose. `TT_DB` has to be a directory owned by the user the daemon runs as,
  that group and others cannot write to, looked at through a descriptor opened
  without following links, in a partition that group and others cannot write to
  or that is sticky; no file of a table may be a link. Requests on any other
  partition fail with `TT_DB_ERR_DB_OPEN_FAILED`.
- tt: fix `TT_MOVE_FILE`. It looked for the mount point of the whole name
  `host:path`, a relative path, and so refused every move, and it named the
  moved file with a `/` after it and the files below it with two. A move inside a
  partition now renames the file and those below it. The tests also cover the
  partitions the server takes from the mount table (the part of
  `isKnownPartition` that no case reached) and the limits of the XDR routines
  when the server encodes a reply.
- tt: tell the caller when a request to the database server fails. After a
  failed request `_Tt_db_object` and `_Tt_db_file` made a new connection and
  returned the result of that, `TT_OK`, in place of the failure: `tt_spec_write`
  of a property value the client could not send returned `TT_OK` and stored
  nothing. They now keep the failure, and a request that cannot be encoded or
  whose reply cannot be decoded is `TT_DB_ERR_RPC_FAILED`, apart from a lost
  connection.
- tt: make the sizes rpc.ttdbserverd accepts the sizes its tables hold. A record
  of the property table is at most `ISMAXRECLEN` bytes and starts with the key
  and the name of the property, but property values and the parts of a queued
  message were allowed to be as long as a record or longer (a value of 1 MiB),
  so values from 32672 bytes and every queued message above that failed inside
  the database, and the sender of a message that could not be queued was told
  `TT_OK`. A value is limited to what is left of a record (32671 bytes on a
  64-bit build; `TT_ERR_PROPLEN` for a spec, refused by libtt before it sends),
  a message is kept in parts of that size and any message up to 16 MiB queues and
  comes back whole, and a message that cannot be queued gives the sender
  `TT_ERR_OVERFLOW` or `TT_ERR_DBFULL`. A file takes no more than 65536 queued
  messages. Taking queued messages compared only the first ptype of a message
  and never stored the ptypes left, so a message queued for several ptypes was
  found by the first only and delivered again to the ptype that had it; the
  parts of a message are removed once the last ptype has it. A netfile may be as
  long as the name `tt_file_netfile` makes of a host and two paths.
- tt: fix `tt_file_move` and `ttmv`: the client stub for `TT_MOVE_FILE` asked for
  `TT_REMOVE_FILE`, so a move removed the file and its objects from the
  database. A test calls every client stub with a client that records the
  procedure asked for. The 16 MiB limit of a message is the marshalled message
  (the arguments and the rest of it), 64 KiB over 16 MiB.
- tt: fix three bugs of the tools. `ttdbck` read a record of the access table
  into a structure that is 12 bytes shorter on a 64-bit build and ended in a
  segmentation fault; `tt_spec_*` on an object that the process had not created
  failed with `TT_ERR_OBJID` on the first lookup because `getSpec` returned a
  shadowed null variable, so `tttar` could not archive objects and `ttcp -p`
  could not copy them; the counter of a key overflowed an `int` for a process id
  above 214748. `tt_spec_write` reports `TT_ERR_PROPLEN`, `TT_ERR_ACCESS`,
  `TT_ERR_DBFULL`, `TT_ERR_DBCONSIST` and `TT_ERR_DBUPDATE` instead of
  `TT_ERR_INTERNAL`.
- tt: fix the move of a file to another partition (`ttmv` across file systems).
  Reading the properties of a key that has none went on to read the next record
  of the property table after the failed positioning, which is an error when the
  table has no position, so a file or object with no properties made the move
  fail with `TT_DB_ERR_CORRUPT_DB` (`TT_ERR_INTERNAL` for the caller); the move
  also used the null list the server answers with for no properties. Tests move
  a file with objects and properties, and one with neither, to another
  partition.
- tt: fix `ttrm -r` and `ttrmdir`. The test of whether a path may be destroyed
  answered no for every path when `-r` was given, so the objects of the tree were
  left in the database; it now answers yes below the path named (never for a
  link), and `ttrmdir` asks for the objects of an empty directory only.
  `tt_file_destroy` of a file the database knows nothing of is `TT_OK`.
- tt: report a denied removal of an object as denied. The server returned the
  path of the file an object is in with the NUL that ends it in the record, so
  it never found the file again by that name: the owner of a file could not
  remove the objects of others in it, and `tt_spec_destroy` by a user who owned
  neither was answered with `TT_ERR_INTERNAL` instead of `TT_ERR_ACCESS`.
- dtlogin: the greeter no longer crashes with SIGSEGV when it exits after a
  login (it closed a message catalog that was not open), and the shipped PAM
  stack marks `pam_lastlog` as an optional session module so a system without
  it logs no error for every login. A test runs the catalog handling in each
  state the greeter can be in at exit (`tests/check-greeter-catalog.py`).
